WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.650
You may have a situation where you have to
print multiple rows of players, almost in

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:16.010
a tab or table format, and you want complete
control of the spacing within the print statement.

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:25.825
I will start another f-string on line 8, and
this time, I will print the player name and

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:26.825
score.

00:00:26.825 --> 00:00:33.200
Then, I will add a colon, a less than symbol,
and 15 to the end of the player1 variable

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.090
to indicate that I want that variable to take
15 spaces and be left-aligned.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:48.340
I will rerun the code, and the last print
statement formats the output as advertised.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:54.079
If I want the score to take 10 spaces and
be right-aligned, what should I do here?

00:00:54.079 --> 00:01:04.379
How about we try a colon, greater than symbol,
and 10 after the score variable.

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:16.780
I will run the code, which seems to work,
but to ensure it works, I will change the

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:22.980
name and the score, rerun the code, and look.

00:01:23.201 --> 00:01:29.109
The spacing and alignment is consistent for
the values in these two outputs.

00:01:30.178 --> 00:01:40.130
The last string format we are going to look
at is akin to the printf function seen in

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:41.130
other programming languages.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.130
Python doesn’t have a defined printf function,
but, it can use a percent symbol along with

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:48.329
a number and letter combination to define
a specific format for text or a number.

00:01:48.329 --> 00:02:00.200
I will type, on line 9, print, and then placeholders
for the Player1 and Score variables.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:06.930
Then, after each colon, I will add the exact
format I want for each variable.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:19.010
I will type %1s after the colon for player1
to indicate one space and a string.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:29.840
After the colon following score, I will type
%.2f to indicate I want the score to show

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.570
with two numbers after a decimal point.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:39.810
By the way, d indicates a whole number and
f indicates a floating-point decimal.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:45.989
The question remains: how do we tell these
placeholders to match up to the player1 and

00:02:45.989 --> 00:02:47.010
score variables?

00:02:47.010 --> 00:03:00.120
After the text, I will add a %, which in this
context is known as the Modulo operator, and

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:09.461
then the order of the variables I want in
parentheses, those being player1 and score.

00:03:09.461 --> 00:03:10.461
Let’s run this code and see the results.

00:03:10.461 --> 00:03:21.850
The last line shows the formatted text as
we wanted in the print statement on line 9.

